    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 315.722, 455.463, 551.584, 526.493 ],
    "font" : "AdvTT31ea7dbe",
    "font size" : 8.5,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "(►Table 3). Full surgical technique details are available in the speciﬁcally referenced articles (e.g., tunnel size, knee ﬁxation angle, order ofgraft ﬁxation). Importantly, the native anatomy guides the desired tunnel locations, and a thorough understanding of the native anatomy is important for a review of postoperative imaging."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 65.708, 467.482, 301.6, 526.492 ],
    "font" : "AdvTT31ea7dbe",
    "font size" : 8.5,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Multiple graft types and ﬁxation devices are used. Ipsilateral knee autografts, including bone-patellar tendon-bone and hamstring tendons, are commonly used for reconstruction grafts in young patients. Allografts are usually reserved for older patients and those with multiple ligament injuries."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
